'''Bayara''' ({{IPA-bl|baˈja.ra}}; {{Langx|ca|Baïara}}; and {{Langx|mxi|بايارا|Bâyârâ}}) is one of the seven [[communities of Balisca|communities]] of the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]]. Of Balisca’s forty-five [[regions of Balisca|constituent regions]], Bayara comprises six: [[Camaca]], [[Xaraba]], [[Carpinchera]], [[Abellesia]], [[Llaragón]] and [[Marjcorzo]]. It is bordered to the north by [[Cerdanya]]'s [[Willi Mapu]], [[Provence]], [[Muntanya]], [[Arcadia]] the east by [[Hesperia]], the south by [[Estuaria]], and to the the southeast by [[Pindorama]] and [[Araucaria]]. Bayara is the largest community within Balisca by area, at {{Convert|1247683.44|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}}, and is the most populous, with an estimated population of 44,042,900 people in 2026.  

Bayara's topography is dominated by the [[Marañón basin]] which takes up roughly 70% of the community's total territory. The relatively flat coast dominates in terms of population and economic resources, and is the cultural and political center from which Bayara expanded following the [[Colonial Balisca|Illypnian colonization of Balisca]]. Due to its expansive [[W:Alluvial plain|alluvial plains]] resulting from the flows of the [[Marañón River]], Bayara is commonly known as the {{W|breadbasket}} of Balisca. The country is bounded by the [[Salsipuedes Sea]] to the west. In the north there are prominent {{W|slope landform|slope}}s near the sea, which also feature diverse environments, such as {{W|Atlantic Coast restingas|restinga vegetation}}, {{W|bay}}s, {{W|lagoon}}s and {{W|tropical forest}}s, which gradually give way to the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Bayaran savanna]] to the south/southeast. 

Before the arrival of Illypnians, the [[Marañón basin]] was home to a collection of Indigenous societies collectively known as the [[Marañón basin civilizations|Marañón cultures]], the largest of which-the [[Tapajóara culture|Tapajóara]]-reached the peak of its development here. These cultures It was known for building large, earthen platform mounds, and often other shaped mounds as well, and their societies were generally composed of a series of urban settlements and [[W:Satellite village|satellite villages]] linked together by loose trading networks. The largest city was [[Ybytyrá-Itá]], believed to be a major religious center, located in what is present-day central [[Xaraba]]. Illypnian exploration began with its discovery by [[Crown of Volisania|Volisan explorer]] [[Raimon de Montcada i Cardona]], who landed at [[Bahia de las Palmas]] on 2 June 1504. Following the issuing of the [[Abyala Charter]](1537), it was disputed between opposing Christian expeditions in what became known as the [[Reapers' War]], from which the Catholic colonists emerged victorious. Bayara was [[Battle of Navassa|the last]] of the republics to join the [[Northern Confederation]], and was the main theatre of action during the [[Baliscan War of Independence|last years of the ''Resistencía'']]. [[Xunta Nacionalista|Bayara would declare it's secession]] from the Confederation in 1828, sparking the [[Baliscan Civil War]]. The Bayaran secessionists were ultimately defeated by 1838 and reintegrated into the confederation.

Today, Bayara is distinctive for its large [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous]] and {{W|expatriate}} communities, high population growth, as well as for its increasing {{W|Climate change|environmental challenges}}. The nation’s economy relies mainly on {{W|Tourism industry|tourism}}, {{W|agriculture industry|agriculture}}, construction and transportation, which developed later than the rest of Balisca in the late 20th century. Bayara is also renowned for its agricultural products, including sugar cane and coffee, its beautiful landscapes, the touristy "[[Solana|Bayaran Riviera]]", and as a popular destination for {{W|Expatriate|retirees}}. Bayara is comprised of some of the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|flattest regions]] of Balisca, with most of the country holding 75% of the {{W|fertile land}} within the Confederation. 

==Etymology==
The name ''Bayara'' is derived from the [[w:Andalusi Arabic|Andalusi Bayani]] word ''al-bayāra'' or ''al-bayyarah'' ({{Langx|ar|البيارة}}), meaning "the [[W:Orchard|orchard]]" or "the [[W:Plantation|plantation]]" (specifically referring to a cultivated grove). This toponym serves as a linguistic fossil of the region's unique colonial demography. Following the [[W:War of the Alpujarras|War of the Alpujarras]] (1568–1571) and the [[Andalusi-Mauretanian wars]] (1609), thousands of [[W:Moriscos|Moriscos]] (Christianized Muslims) and other Andalusi were forcibly resettled in the territory under the [[Volisanian charter system]], led by the [[Levantine Brotherhood|''Ermandad Libánti'']] ("Levantine Brotherhood"). These settlers applied the Bayani term to the fertile, Mesogean-like coastal plains where they established the archipelago's first large-scale agricultural estates.

The name proved prophetic, as Bayara subsequently developed into the center of the colonial [[W:Plantation economy|plantation economy]], dominating the production of [[W:Sugar|sugar]], [[W:Brazilwood|brazilwood]], and [[W:Coffee|coffee]]. While the name was adapted into [[Xacano language|Jacano]] orthography (often appearing as ''A Baiara'' in early colonial manuscripts, reflecting the definite article), the phonetic structure remained largely unchanged from its Bayani root. This distinguishes Bayara from other Baliscan regions, whose names are typically derived from the [[W:Tupi-Guarani languages|Kurupiran-Nyengatú languages]] ([[Araucaria]]), [[Ajami]], [[W:Catalan language|Volisanian]] (e.g., [[Cerdanya]]), or Catholic liturgy (e.g., [[Sant Chuan]]).

==History==

=== Pre-history and Marañón basin civilizations ===
{{Main|Marañón basin civilizations}}

Human settlement of the Marañón basin dates to at least 10,000 years before present, with the earliest archaeological evidence concentrated in the highlands of [[Xaraba]] and the upper [[Marañón River]] valley. Pottery fragments radiocarbon-dated to approximately 8,000 years old—excavated near [[Ka'aÿra]] in the former [[Aruanãyára Empire]]—demonstrate that the tropical forest regions of the basin supported complex prehistoric cultures far earlier than previously assumed.

By the late pre-contact period, the basin had developed into one of [[Adonia]]'s independent [[W:Cradle of civilization|cradles of civilization]], distinguished by its riverine urbanism: settlements concentrated along the Marañón and its tributaries, linked by canoe trade networks that extended from the coastal estuaries to the interior highlands. The [[Tapajóara culture]] emerged as the dominant polity in the lower basin, controlling territory from present-day [[Camaca]] to central [[Xaraba]] through a combination of agricultural intensification, riverine commerce, and military coercion. The Tapajóara built large earthen platform mounds—some exceeding 20 hectares in area—that served as ceremonial centers, elite residences, and fortified enclosures. Their largest city, [[Ybytyrá-Itá]], functioned as a major religious and political center, with a population estimated at between 20,000 and 40,000 at its peak.

The [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] formed a sharp cultural boundary between the settled agrarian societies of the eastern littoral and the semi-nomadic peoples of the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]. West of the mountains, the [[Kurupiranga]] peoples—the [[Guanarú]], [[Ypérynã]], [[Honú-Abá]], and others—maintained mobile subsistence patterns based on hunting, fishing, and shifting cultivation, organized into kin-based bands that occasionally coalesced into larger confederations for mutual defense or seasonal resource exploitation.

The [[Aruanãyára Empire]] (c. 1460–1520) extended its influence into the eastern Marañón watershed through trade, tribute extraction, and intermittent military campaigns, though the basin's dense forests and flood-prone rivers limited the empire's ability to project power as effectively as it did in the mountain highlands. Aruanãyára administrative concepts—including the labor obligation and the road system—were adopted in modified form by the Tapajóara and other basin cultures, and [[Classical Aruanãyára]] served as a diplomatic and commercial ''[[W:Lingua franca|lingua franca]]'' across much of the eastern watershed.

Population estimates for the pre-contact Marañón basin remain contested, but most scholars place the figure between 5 and 10 million at its peak—densities that would not be reached again until the 20th century. Unlike the catastrophic demographic collapses that accompanied Illypnian contact in other parts of the Algarves, the Marañón basin's Indigenous populations had been exposed to a range of Old World pathogens through centuries of contact with Oceanian peoples crossing the Shendan Ocean. While the Illypnian introduction of novel diseases still caused significant mortality—particularly in dense urban centers like Ybytyrá-Itá—the prior exposure had produced a degree of population-level immunity that mitigated the worst effects of virgin soil epidemics. The primary drivers of Indigenous population decline in the basin were instead enslavement, territorial displacement, and the disruption of subsistence systems under colonial rule.

===Illypnian arrival, exploration and settlement===
{{Main|Volisanian charter system|Captaincy General of the Marañón}}

The Volisanian discovery of the Bayaran coast is attributed to explorer [[Raimon de Montcada i Cardona]], who made landfall at [[Bahia de las Palmas]] on 2 June 1504. Initial contact was followed by decades of desultory exploration, as the Volisanian Crown prioritized the extraction of [[W:Brasilwood|brasilwood]] and other coastal resources over the establishment of permanent settlements. The [[Abyala Charter]] of 1537 formalized the Volisanian claims and introduced the [[Volisanian charter system|charter system]]—a framework of territorial concessions granted to private entrepreneurs who were expected to finance their own expeditions, establish settlements, and share revenues with the Crown. The [[Captaincy General of the Marañón]] was created to administer the basin concessions, but effective colonial control remained confined to the coastal fringe for much of the 16th century, as the interior remained under the sway of the [[Tapajóara culture|Tapajóara]] (amongst other [[Kurupiranga|Kurupiran peoples]]) and the emerging quilombos.

The colonial population was divided from the outset along ethnoreligious lines. The Jacano-speaking [[W:Conquistadors|conquistadors]], predominantly [[Petran Catholic Church|Catholic]], drove the expansion of the plantation economy and the enslavement of Indigenous and Kaftian peoples. The Volisanian-speaking [[Catharism|Cathars]], who had been resettled in Balisca as part of the Crown's policy of religious colonization, opposed the enslavement of Indigenous peoples on religious grounds—a position that placed them in direct conflict with the Jacano planter class and established the fundamental rift that would shape Bayaran politics for the next three centuries.

The introduction of enslaved Kaftians—many of whom were Muslim war captives with military experience—proved destabilizing to the colonial order. Despite Volisanian ordinances as early as 1521 limiting the importation of Muslim Kaftians, the Jacano colonies' demand for labor outweighed security concerns. Repeated rebellions by enslaved Kaftians prevented colonial settlement from expanding beyond the coastal zone and gave rise to the [[quilombo]] communities—fortified settlements of escaped and freed Kaftians—that would evolve into the [[Maroon Confederacy]].

====Cathar-Catholic conflict====
{{Main|Reapers' War}}

The '''Reapers' War''' (1578–1608) was the defining conflict of the colonial period, pitting Jacano-speaking Catholic colonists against Volisanian-speaking Catharist communities and their respective Indigenous allies. The war's immediate trigger was a dispute over land rights in the lower Marañón, but its roots lay in the fundamental incompatibility of the two colonial projects: the Jacano plantation system, dependent on enslaved labor, versus the Catharist reductions, which offered sanctuary to Indigenous peoples fleeing enslavement. Jacano militias targeted Catharist settlements and reductions, destroying villages and enslaving Indigenous residents, while Catharist forces—aided by their Indigenous allies and, increasingly, by Maroon raiders from the quilombos—conducted retaliatory campaigns against plantation estates.

The [[Peace of Santa Creu]] (1608) ended the fighting but confirmed the Catharist defeat. The treaty resulted in the [[Tarraco exodus|expulsion of Cathars]] from the Marañón basin, the consolidation of Catholic Jacano control over Bayara, and the establishment of the [[Cerdanya#Catharist settlement|Colony of Cerdanya]] as a Catharist refuge in the north. The partition of the territory along ethnoreligious lines—the Catholic Jacano south and the Catharist Volisanian north—remained a defining feature of Bayaran political geography until the [[Baliscan Revolution]].

The Reapers' War coincided with the broader forced migration of [[Moriscos]] from the Illypnian peninsula. The [[W:War of the Alpujarras|War of the Alpujarras]] (1568–1571) and the [[W:Expulsion of the Moriscos|Expulsion of the Moriscos]] (1609) dispersed over three million Moriscos across the Volisanian Empire, many of whom were resettled in Bayara under the terms of the [[Peace of Granada]] and subsequent Volisanian relocation programs. The influx of [[Mozbayani]]-speaking Christians and Muslims—offered lands of three times the acreage they relinquished in Illypnia—further complicated Bayara's ethnoreligious landscape and contributed to the syncretic culture that would later distinguish [[Bayaran Jacano]] from its metropolitan source.

====Maroon rebellions and quilombos====
{{Main|Maroon Wars|Maroon pirates}}
[[File:Mapa da Capitania de Pernambuco, com representação do Quilombo dos Palmares (1647).jpg|thumb|1647 map of the [[Province of Pernambuco]], in modern-day region of [[Xaraba]], with representation of the  ''Quilombo de Palmares'']]
The first slave rebellions began almost immediately following the introduction of slavery in Bayara (1526). Rebellions in the Northern Triangle differed from other slave revolts in the colonial world due to the role of the [[Catharism|Catharist]] communities of [[Cerdanya]], whose early abolition of slavery in 1611 created a [[W:Slave states and free states|territorial sanctuary]] for self-liberating people and whose missionary network provided the literacy, material support, and theological framework that supported the Maroon resistance to expand from isolated revolts into a coordinated revolutionary movement. The convergence of Cathar literacy campaigns with the existing literacy of enslaved [[Islam in Balisca|Muslim]] Kaftians—estimated at sixty percent of the enslaved population—produced a uniquely capable resistance network that could communicate, plan, and administer across the basin's difficult terrain.

The [[Peace of Santa Creu]] (1608) had expelled the Cathars from the Marañón basin, but the establishment of the [[Colony of Cerdanya]] as a Catharist refuge in the north proved decisive for the Maroon cause. In 1611, the [[Captaincy General of Cerdanya]]—under the influence of its Cathar merchant and religious elite—outlawed {{W|chattel slavery}} within its jurisdiction, declaring that "no human soul may be held as property, for the spirit is the domain of God alone and the body its temporary vessel." The decree transformed Cerdanya into a territorial sanctuary for self-liberating people. Runaway slaves who reached Cerdanya were immediately declared free, and Cerdanyan authorities refused all requests from Bayaran planters for the return of fugitives.

Cathar theology held slavery to be a {{W|mortal sin}}—a manifestation of the material world's corruption of the spirit. The [[Treatise on the Just Resistance]] ({{lang|oc|Tractat de la Justa Resisténcia}}), composed by the [[W:Cathar Perfect|Cathar perfectus]] [[Bernat de Calvià]] around 1580, provided the theological framework that justified and motivated Cathar support for the Maroon resistance, distinguishing between the initiation of violence (which was sinful) and the resistance to oppression (which was a spiritual obligation). Cathar missionaries operating clandestinely in the Bayaran interior taught literacy to enslaved communities, distributed the ''Treatise'', and maintained communication networks between the quilombos and Cerdanya.

The Cathar literacy campaigns converged with the existing literacy of enslaved Muslim Kaftians, many of whom could read and write in the Bayani script. This combined literacy enabled communication across plantations, the forging of passes and freedom documents, and the preservation of military and strategic knowledge in written form—capabilities that colonial authorities repeatedly attempted and failed to suppress. The result was a resistance network of unprecedented organizational sophistication, capable of coordinating actions across the vast distances and difficult terrain of the [[Marañón basin]]. This, combined with the growing strength of the insurgent [[Maroon Confederacy]] progressively undermined the Bayaran plantation economy. Maroon corsairs intercepted slave ships with growing frequency, freeing their captives and driving up the risks of the slave trade. 

=== Independence movements and revolution ===
{{Main|United Provinces of Bayara|Volisanya Boba}}

The [[secession of Reige]] from the United Kingdoms in 1736 triggered the [[Great Volisan Civil War]], a 65-year metropolitan crisis that transformed Baliscan colonial governance. With Volisania consumed by the conflict and its eventual resolution—the [[W:Devolution|devolution]] of the Kingdoms into its present constituent states under the [[Law of the Community of Volisania and Status of the Crown Acts 1801 and 1812]]—the Baliscan concessions entered the period known as ''Volisanya Boba'' ("meek Volisania"). During this seven-decade interval, the colonies operated with minimal metropolitan oversight, developing autonomous institutions, commercial networks, and a distinctly "proto-Baliscan" political identity. The term, which emerged in the 1740s among colonial administrators, captured both the weakness of Volisanian authority and the opportunity this presented for colonial self-determination.

During this period, the [[United Provinces of Bayara|United Provinces]] emerged as the dominant political entity in the [[Marañón basin]]. Initially a composite constitutional monarchy under rival claimants to the Volisanian crown—[[Pere V of Volisania|Pere V]] (Traditionalist, [[House of Domènech]]) and [[Ramon VI of Volisania|Ramon VI]] (Reformist, [[House of Montcada]])—the United Provinces transitioned to a republic governed by the [[Junta of Sant Agostín]] following the declaration of sovereignty in 1743. The [[Cismarañón expedition]] of the 1760s extended Bayaran control to the [[Cismarañón Province|north bank of the Marañón]], and the republic's capital at [[Macaio]] became the commercial hub of the basin.

The collapse of Volisanian naval protection during the civil war had immediate consequences for Bayara's plantation economy. The intensification of [[Maroon pirates|Maroon piracy]]—operating from bases in the [[Maroon Confederacy]] and the [[Cabu Verd]] archipelago—made the Abyalan slave routes increasingly dangerous. Maroon corsairs intercepted slave ships with growing frequency, liberating their captives and driving up the cost and risk of the Kaftian trade. Deprived of Volisanian naval protection and facing a labor crisis on the plantations, Bayara's planter class pivoted westward toward the Shendan Ocean, seeking alternative sources of unfree labor.

The result was a trade in [[W:Blackbirding|blackbirded]] Fosians and Tautai—primarily [[Dagit|Dagiti]], [[Sultanate of Maros|Marosian]], and [[Tautai peoples|Tautai]] peoples kidnapped or deceived into indentured servitude by Fosian and Sanese labor recruiters operating in the waters around the [[Dagit|Dagiti archipelago]], the [[Balausia|Balausian Peninsula]], and the far-flung islands of "[[Polynesia]]". Bayaran merchants, already embedded in the Shendan commercial networks that had developed on the back of the [[Cinc Parets-Macaio galleon trade]], served as intermediaries: the port of [[Macaio]] received shipments of blackbirded Fosians, and the planters purchased them through the same private channels that supplied Shendan commodities. The trade operated in the legal ambiguity created by the Volisanya Boba: the collapse of Volisanian colonial authority meant there was no metropolitan power to enforce even the nominal prohibitions that existed, and the Zong Edict banning slavery on the mainland did not explicitly address overseas territories. The Bayaran planters exploited this ambiguity with the same pragmatism that characterized their commercial pivot to the Shendan: when the Abyalan trade became untenable, they found an alternative supply.

The Shendan labor trade was smaller in scale than the Kaftian trade it supplemented—perhaps 50,000 to 100,000 blackbirded Fosians and Tautai were brought through Macaio between 1740 and 1790, compared to the hundreds of thousands of enslaved Kaftians who had preceded them—but its moral and political consequences were disproportionate. The trade declined in the 1780s and 1790s as Zong naval enforcement, Marosian resistance in their peripheral territories, and the self-reproducing enslaved population in the Fosian colonies reduced the supply of newly imported captives. By the time of the [[Granyana Convention]] in 1809, the Shendan trade had effectively ceased—though the Dagiti, Marosian, and Tautai descendants of the blackbirded peoples remained in Bayara, forming a distinct community within the broader Kafto-Baliscan population and maintaining cultural traditions that differentiated them from the descendants of the older Kaftian diaspora.

===== ''Volisanyado'' and internal turmoil =====
{{Main|Bayaran Civil Wars|Reconquesta}}

The waning years of the Volisanya Boba period saw the outbreak of the [[Bayaran Civil Wars]]—a series of internal conflicts between competing factions within the United Provinces—and the first permanently successful overthrow of monarchical colonial rule in Balisca. These conflicts were exacerbated by the emergence of the ''[[Volisanyado]]'' ({{Langx|an|Bolisanyáus}}, {{Literal translation|Volisanianized ones}})—a term that originally designated Bayaran elites who adopted Volisanian metropolitan culture, fashions, and political attitudes, but which evolved into a pejorative label for those who collaborated with the Volisanian Commonwealth's attempt to reassert imperial authority over the Baliscan colonies.

The term ''Volisanyado'' surfaced during the early decades of the Volisanya Boba, initially carrying a neutral or mildly aspirational meaning: it described Bayaran merchants, intellectuals, and plantation owners who maintained commercial and cultural ties with the metropole and who saw Volisanian governance as a vehicle for enlightened reform and modernization. As the ''Volisanya Boba'' progressed and colonial autonomy deepened, the term acquired a more specific and increasingly negative connotation, referring to those who advocated for the restoration of metropolitan authority—often as a means of preserving the institution of slavery, which was under increasing pressure from Catharist abolitionism and the growing strength of the [[Maroon Confederacy]].

The ambitions of the Volisanian Centralists found willing collaborators among the Bayaran economic elite following the conclusion of the [[Volisanian Civil War]] in 1801. The ''[[Restaurasionistas]]'' faction, led by Prime Minister [[Enric de Montclar]], sought to reverse the decades of colonial autonomy that had characterized the Volisanya Boba period and reintegrate all colonial possessions directly into the Commonwealth structure. Montclar's government, dominated by metropolitan interests who had suffered immensely during the civil war, viewed the recovery of colonial revenues as essential to the Commonwealth's financial stability. Many plantation owners aligned with the metropolitan government in the hopes of preserving the institution of slavery upon which their profitable plantation economy was based. Slavery was under increasing pressure in light of growing abolitionist sentiment among Hesperian and Cerdanyan republicans. The ideological rift intensified in reaction to the [[Granyana Convention]] of 1806, in which the emergent republics pledged to "fully eradicate" the practice and redistribute land to freedmen. The prospect of losing both property and political dominance galvanized many Bayaran landowners, who viewed Volisanian intervention as a means to restore a social and economic order increasingly under threat. However, beyond this alliance of convenience, the Bayarans had little affection for metropolitan authority.

In September 1808, Bayara's legislature—the [[General Junta (Bayara)|General Junta]]—dominated by plantation owners and metropolitan sympathizers, issued a proclamation of allegiance to the "legitimate government of the Community of Volisania," citing the need for "stability, order, and the defense of property and civilization." Shortly thereafter, the Junta granted permission for Volisanian expeditionary forces—dispatched from metropolitan garrisons—to land at key Bayaran ports, including [[Sant Chuan d'os Baxos]] and [[Navassa]]. These landings marked the first foreign military incursion into Baliscan territory in nearly a century and represented the beginning of Volisania's attempt to reassert imperial authority. [[Hesperia]] and [[Cerdanya]] denounced the move as a betrayal of their longstanding political independence and their opposition to metropolitan centralization. In response to the invasion, eighteen colonial communities issued the [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]] in 1809, uniting as the [[Northern Confederation]] and formally declaring resistance to Volisanian reconquest—officially launching the [[Baliscan War of Independence]].

The collabortion of the ''Volisanyado'' was not monolithic. A minority—primarily intellectuals and merchants with genuine ties to metropolitan culture—sincerely believed that Volisanian governance offered the best path to modernization and stability. The majority, however, were pragmatic collaborators: plantation owners who calculated that metropolitan authority was the "lesser evil" compared to abolition and land redistribution. This distinction was largely lost on the Bayaran populace, for whom the term ''Volisanyado'' became synonymous with treason against Bayaran self-determination.
[[File:Bombardment of Navassa.jpg|thumb|''[[Bombardeig de Navassa]]'' - [[Eloi Vila Dalmau]] (1868) depicting the [[Battle of Navassa|defeat of the Volisan garrison at Navassa]]]]
In 1816, representatives of Royalist Bayara surrendered to the confederal forces following the decisive [[Battle of Navassa]], effectively destroying the last royalist strongholds in mainland Balisca, allowing the rebels to consolidate ending the threat of invasion against the Confederation. While this did not change the position of Volisania against colonial emancipation, it effectively forced Volisania to abandon its aspirations for an imperial ''[[Reconquesta]]''. Over the course of the next two years, anti-royalist forcers won major victories to consolidate their control in their respective provinces, and threatened [[Patriotic State of Potocsí|Volisan loyalist strongholds in Potocsí]]. 

In the war's aftermath, the Volisanyado were subjected to a campaign of persecution: those who had held office under the Volisanian administration, accepted metropolitan honors, or collaborated with the expeditionary forces were imprisoned, exiled, or executed. An estimated 4,000 to 12,000 Volisanyado and their families fled to Volisania with the retreating expeditionary forces.

===Post-independence===
{{See also|Pastry War|Confederal Question}}
[[File:Zouave - Grolleron crop.jpg|thumb|328x328px|Cerdanyan ''Zuau'' in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], <abbr>c.</abbr> 1825]]
Independence also did not result in the end of political turmoil, and was immediately followed by infighting. During the war, confederal forces had imprisoned, hanged, or expelled all former slave owners (most relocated to [[Potocsí]]), and divided their land and other assets between the newly emancipated Kaftian population. Most of the landowners that had supported the royalists were temporarily disenfranchised.

This further hardened attitudes in Bayara against confederation, which already was strong due to the devastation in Bayara in terms of infrastructure and the economy. Because of the reluctance of the land-owning class accept the loss of their privileges and to grant voting rights to freedmen, the Confederal Congress adopted [[Reconstruction laws]] over Bayaran objections. In response, and intending to avoid the social effects of the war, most of the Bayaran provinces passed [[W:Black codes|black codes]]. In the early years of confederation, these were mostly ''de jure'' nullified by confederal law and pro-reformist legislatures, but widely continued to be enforced by Bayaran officials.

Growing calls for the strengthening of confederal power and increasing confederal interference in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]] fueled [[Bayaran alienation|Bayaran resentment]]. Intercommunal violence between predominately Volisanian-speaking Cathars and Jacon-speaking Catholics erupted following the murder of a [[W:Pastry|pastry]] chef in [[Xàbia]] by a Catholic mob, resulting in [[Pastry War|escalating reprisals and counter-reprisals]] which eventually culminated in [[Confederal emergency|confederal intervention]] and the raising of the [[Cerdanyan Legion]] as a peacekeeping force to administer the Estuarian regions. It was ultimately ended by the [[Navassan Charter]]. 

The Charter led to the ''de facto'' partition of Bayara on [[W:Ethnoreligious group|ethnoreligious]] lines, establishing military districts which were propped up by the presence of the [[Cerdanyan Legion]] (popularly known as ''zuau''). The ''Zuau'' were relatively effective in preventing further violence, but, they faced significant opposition from Bayaran political leaders who resented confederal interference in what they considered local affairs. Bayaran delegates to the Confederal Congress argued that the Legion represented an unconstitutional occupation of Bayaran territory and a violation of provincial autonomy. Hostility towards the ''Zuau'' was also widespread in the [[Christianity in Balisca|Catholic Church]] and amongst it's followers, who viewed the predominately [[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzan]] force as "Muslim occupiers", and [[W:Islamophobic trope|Islamophobic tropes]] rapidly spread across Bayaran communities, with false allegations that the ''Zuau'' were committing atrocities against Catholics, especially women. Bayaran dissatisfaction with the terms of the Treaty led to it's delegates blocking the allocation of confederal funding for the Legion in the [[Confederal Congress]], ultimately forcing its withdrawal and disbandment in the spring of 1826.

====Civil war and reconstruction====
{{Main|Baliscan Civil War|Anyos Federales|Maroon removal}}

In response to increasing unrest, Hesperian delegates convened the [[1826 Confederal Convention]], which produced the [[Compromise of 1827]]. This agreement proposed a significant overhaul of the Articles of Confederation, replacing them with a federated charter that would grant greater powers to a centralized government, including fiscal authority, judicial oversight, and an integrated military structure. Delegates representing Hesperia and Cerdanya supported the compromise almost unanimously, while the Bayaran provinces (with the exception of the [[Carpinchera Republic|Province of Macaio]]) boycotted the convention in favor of secession from the confederation. When the [[Xunta Nacionalista]] formally denounced the Articles on 6 July 1828, the Confederal Congress ordered a mobilization for war.
[[File:Flag_of_Carpinchera.svg|left|thumb|The flag of the [[Carpinchera Republic]], now adopted as the flag of the [[Carpinchera|region of Carpinchera]]]]
The city of [[Macaio]], which economically dominated the Republic and generally favored [[Confederal emergency|confederal intervention]], refused to recognize the Xunta. Urban Macaio's commercial interests, tied to the entrepôt trade and the [[Hesperian League]]'s commercial networks, were fundamentally at odds with the agrarian nationalism of the Xunta. The [[Carpinchera Republic]], proclaimed on 6 July 1828 under President [[Martín Ferrández]], was a pro-confederation, anti-separatist breakaway state that militarily controlled Macaio and its immediate hinterland for fourteen months. Though never explicitly recognized by the Confederation, the Carpinchera Republic played a key role in disrupting the secessionist war effort in the opening months.
[[File:Guerillas_rugendas.jpg|left|thumb|''Bandeirantes'' by [[Johann Moritz Rugendas]], depicting an episode of the [[Maroon removal]] policy]]
The most consequential act of the Xunta Nacionalista was the adoption of the [[Maroon resettlement|Maroon removal]] as official state policy. Building on decades of settler pressure and the expansionist desire to acquire Indigenous land in the Abyalan interior, the Xunta formalized the forced displacement of approximately 150,000 freedmen and free persons of color, along with 60,000 members of the Honú-Abá, Murí, Aruanãyará, Roáka'á, and Kalútupí nations, expelling them to the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. The removal, which modern scholarship characterizes as ethnic cleansing and settler genocide, was driven by anti-Black and anti-Indigenous racism, Christian supremacy, and the desire to erase the social restructuring imposed by abolition. It intensified the confederal crisis, ultimately triggering the [[Basilio mutiny]] and the outbreak of the [[Baliscan Civil War]].
[[File:Banks’s_Expedition;_burning_of_the_state_capitol_of_Louisiana,_Baton_Rouge,_Tuesday_night,_December_30th,_1862.jpg|thumb|[[Burning of Uxiu|Destruction]] of the [[Cabildo of Uxiu]] on the night of 6 June 1837 by Confederal forces]]
Despite controlling a numerical troop advantage throughout the war, the Bayarans were forced to divert significant numbers of troops towards their efforts to subdue the intensifying uprisings and ambushes by Maroons and [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous peoples]] resisting the Xunta's policy of [[Maroon resettlement|forced removal]] from the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]. Secessionist forces would go on to wage of violent campaign of expulsion, in the process successfully destroying dozens of ''quilombos'' and expelling their inhabitants to the territory of the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. Notably however, Bayaran armies crossed the [[Angostura]] and attempted to capture the [[Alpujarra]], culminating in the [[Siege of Castejón]], which was beaten back with the intervention of the [[Maroon Confederacy]] in support of pro-confederation forces. By 1835, most Bayaran pro-secessionist land and naval forces had either disbanded, surrendered or otherwise ceased hostilities.

Despite this the war lacked a formal end, with bands of secessionist guerrilla forces fighting, surrendering or disbanding sporadically throughout most of 1835-1837. Tensions reignited after a massacre in [[Brumavent]], in which pro-secessionist soldiers executed the hundreds of captives and vowed to continue their guerrilla warfare. This triggered significant unrest on both sides and reignited the already high tensions between the Volisanian-speaking and Jacano-speaking communities that resided in the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]. Reprisals and counter reprisals escalated, and the two factions subsequently fought a series of battles across the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], from which the pro-confederation forces again emerged victorious. Bayara suffered substantially more than the rest of the confederation overall, as the federalist strategy of [[W:Attrition warfare|attrition warfare]] meant that Bayaran forces could not replace their casualties, and the total war waged by Hesperian armies devastated the infrastructure and caused widespread poverty and distress.

The Carpinchera Republic and the rest of Bayara were reintegrated into the Northern Confederation after the secessionists' defeat at the [[Battle of Punta Lagunas]] in 1837. The subsequent [[Treaty of Bixessarri]] (1838) punished Bayara severely: the republic was forced to pay the expenses and debts incurred by the Confederation during the war, the General Junta was coerced into ratifying the Compromise of 1827, and significant amounts of land in Bayara's north were ceded — including [[Trás-as-Montanyas]] (ceded to Hesperia) and the northern wetlands (allocated to the now-independent Estuarian community). The treaty also revoked the Maroon removal policy, facilitated the [[W:Right of return|right of return]] for displaced populations, and mandated reparations in the form of [[W:Land redistribution|land redistribution]] — the very redistribution that Bayara's planter class had seceded to prevent. The recognition of [[W:Indigenous sovereignty|Indigenous sovereignty]] and constitutional guarantees of cultural and political rights for all communities, backed by aggressive confederal protection, completed the dismantling of the racial hierarchy that had defined Bayaran society. 

=== Industrialization'', Comuncazo'' and political dissolution ===
{{Main|Baliscan Revolution}}

Bayara's industrial development, from 1840 into the 1930s, was gradual and inward looking. Initially, industry was closely associated with agriculture: cotton plantations led to the growth of [[W:Textile manufacturing|textile manufacturing]]. Coffee planters were among the early industrial investors. However, this rapid, export-driven growth led to increasing societal stratification. The [[Volisan aristocracy]] wielded disproportionate economic and political power at the republican and confederal level, causing simmering unrest amongst the Bayaran populace. Restrictions on imports forced by the First World War and government policies of "import substitution" and trade tariffs, all contributed to industrial growth.

The boom in immigration provided a market for goods, and sectors such as food processing grew. However the rapidly growing urban population grew increasingly resentful of the economic elite, which had combined its economic power with the electoral strength of immigrant communities and the employing of [[W:Machine politics|machine politics]] to maintain their dominant position. The stand-off was also political: politics had been long monopolized by the [[Bayaran Republican Party]] (PRB), but by the eve of the Baliscan Revolution, left-wing ([[Anarchism in Balisca|predominately anarchist]]) movements had been growing in opposition and the country was increasingly poised for violent unrest. The working conditions and the oligarchic political system of the industrialization era, which made it difficult for workers to participate, meant that anarchism quickly gained strength among workers. [[W:Revolutionary syndicalism|Revolutionary syndicalism]] exerted a great influence on the workers' movement, especially at workers' congresses and in the strikes of the period. Anarchists also contributed to the creation of a series of periodicals for the workers' press and founded several Modern Schools around the country.

The prolongation of the war in Europe helped to bring about, in 1916, an explosion of industrial production. As imported products stopped reaching Bayaran ports, national industries returned to employing workers to meet growing demand. However, there was a considerable increase in the cost of living, given that several products were exported to countries in conflict. In 1916, the prices of rice, sugar and corn increased significantly; the following year, the wholesale prices of various products went up, such as beans and [[W:Cassava|manioc flour]]; and wheat became scarce and expensive. Wages, however, remained at the same levels as in the years before the war. Food prices continued to rise during the first six months of 1917. Basic items tended to be 20% to 150% more expensive than the previous year. The favorable environment for the satisfaction of demands due to the resumption of industrial activity led the labor movement to an accelerated process of reorganization in disarticulated resistance societies during the crisis, resulting in a strike wave starting in 1917.

In 1917, the PRB attempted to amend the republic's constitution in order to reinforce the existing socio-economic dynamic and thwart the growing radicalism. 
[[File:Revolução de 1930 (18).jpg|thumb|Bayaran strikers in Macaio celebrating the coup against the Confederal Congress during the Baliscan Revolution]]
The contentious [[1918 Cerdanyan general election]] saw the victory of Pol Pérez, a [[Progress Party (Cerdanya)|notorious crony capitalist]]. His election, widely condemned as fraudulent, sparked outrage across the confederation. Left-wing activists demanded the overthrow of the aristocracy and a re-run of the election, a call that quickly spread across the nation. On 28 October 1918, over four million students, laborers, and union members took to the streets in what would become known as the [[1918 Baliscan general strike]]. 

The movement escalated on 21 April 1919, with the [[storming of the Baliscan Cabildo]] by rebelling soldiers supported by civilians in reaction to increasingly violent repression by the state. The successful coup against the Confederal Congress and the seizure the Cabildo [[W:Power of the deed|instigated]] the second, more violent phase of the Baliscan Revolution, ultimately leading to a significant rise in {{W|political violence}} nationwide. Tensions further escalated with the assassination of Deputy Premier [[Moisès Vilchez]] by [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] [[Miquel Cebrián]]. With the country on the verge of a violent {{W|civil war}}, the Council of Ministers narrowly voted against {{W|vote of no confidence|confidence}} in Premier [[Máximo Burjassot]], forcing the administration to dissolve. Faced with continued unrest on the streets and an increasingly demoralized and hostile military, the Confederal Council called a [[1919 Confederal Convention|Confederal Convention]].

The political revolution that followed was transformative. It resulted in the abolition of the confederal republican system and implemented {{W|anarchism|anarchist}} and {{W|Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist}} principles. Internal borders were abolished, secularization was enacted, and {{W|Socialist self-management}} took root. Araucaria’s economy, like much of Balisca’s, was transformed with significant portions placed under {{W|worker control}}. Agrarian areas were collectivized and operated as libertarian socialist communes, while social welfare programs were expanded through income and {{W|wealth redistribution}}. Socioeconomic policies were heavily influenced by the {{W|Italian Futurism|Italian}} and {{W|Russian Futurism|Russian Futurist}} movements, eventually coalescing into an [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] ideology. This period also accelerated the process of {{W|indigenous decolonization}} that had begun with the [[1883 Confederal Convention]]. 

