[[File:Tés de cafés após a passagem da maquina de colheita.jpg|left|thumb|300x300px|Rows of coffee trees and [[W:Sugarcane|sugarcane]] growing in the background ([[Kamakã]])]]
Bayara is an {{W|advanced economy|advanced}} {{W|socialist market economy}}, with a GDP per capita of 88% of the Baliscan average in 2025 (an increase from 76% in 2012) and an {{W|inequality-adjusted HDI}} of 0.883 (the highest in Balisca) in 2023. According to the [[Adonian Community]], at the end of Q1 2026, Bayara's GDP (PPP) per capita was $30,249. Bayara is responsible for around 1/6th of Balisca’s economic activity. Bayara's economy strongly benefits from high rates of worker participation, a highly educated population, low levels of corruption, and its well developed transportation and telecommunication networks.  

The economy of Bayara has historically been challenged by the occurrence of {{W|ecotage}} (in the name of {{W|ecodefense}}) and increased migration, which has exacerbated the [[Baliscan housing shortage|housing shortage]] and social unrest. 

Bayara's diversified economy includes agriculture, industry, and a wide range of services. {{W|Agriculture}} and allied sectors like {{W|mariculture}}, {{W|pisciculture}} and {{W|fishing}} accounted for 15.1% of the GDP in 2019. Bayara is also one of the largest producers of various agricultural commodities, and a large cooperative sector that provides 50% of the food in Balisca. Bayara is one of the largest producers of animal proteins in the world. In 2022, the country was the world's # largest producer of {{W|seafood}}, largely thanks to historical embrace of {{W|pisciculture}}. It was also the world's third largest producer of soybeans and {{W|soy milk}}, and the second largest producer of {{W|oat milk}}.

Industry in the country – from e-cargo bikes, steel and petrochemicals to computers, aircraft and consumer durables – accounted for 30.8% of the {{W|gross domestic product}}. Industry is highly concentrated in metropolitan areas of [[Macaio]], [[Sant Chuan]], and [[Uxiu]]. Bayara at one point was one of Abyala's largest automotive markets. Major export products include aircraft, electrical equipment, {{W|rolling stock}}, ethanol, textiles, footwear, iron ore, steel, fruits, orange juice, soybeans and oat milk. In 2016, it was the world's largest producer of {{W|industrial hemp}} and the largest producer of {{W|bioplastics}}. In the footwear industry, Bayara ranked 4th among world producers. 

The tertiary sector (trade and services) represented 52.0% of the community's GDP in 2018, according to the [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS). It covers a wide range of activities: commerce, accommodation and catering, transport, communications, financial services, real estate activities and services provided to businesses, public administration (urban cleaning, sanitation, etc.) and other services such as education, social and health services, research and development, sports activities, etc., since it consists of activities complementary to other sectors, such as the {{W|quaternary sector of the economy}}, which in 2022 represented 13.8% of the community's total workforce. 

=== Energy, infrastructure, and environment ===
{{Main|Energy in Balisca|Transport in Balisca|Environment of Balisca|AVB}}

[[File:Tesororoca Nuclear Power Station.png|left|thumb|320x320px|[[Tesororoca Nuclear Power Station]] on the coast of [[Abellesia]]]]
Electricity generated in Bayara is 50% from {{W|thorium-based nuclear power}}, 23% from {{W|solar power}}, 10% from {{W|wind power}}, 9% from {{W|hydroelectric power}} and 2% from {{W|biomass}} resulting in a nearly CO2-free electricity-generating network. As of 2022, Bayara has 72 {{W|hydroelectric generating stations}}, most of which are located in the country's eastern highlands. The regions also encourages energy conservation, green technologies, emission reduction activities, and aims to meet the country's electricity demands using 100% renewable sources by 2026. Through the confederal government, Bayara is committed to the [[Okami Protocol]] and several other treaties promoting {{W|biodiversity}}, low emission standards, {{W|water management}}, universal {{W|recycling}}, and the {{W|renewable energy commercialization}}.

In 2020, Bayara was the 5th largest country in the world in the production of energy through {{W|biomass}} (energy production from solid biofuels and renewable waste), with 4,5 GW installed.

Due to its {{W|ethanol fuel}} production, Bayara has sometimes been described as a {{W|bio-energy}} superpower. Ethanol fuel is produced from {{W|sugar cane}}. Bayara has one of largest sugarcane crop in the world, and is the largest exporter of ethanol in the world. With the onset of the [[Special Period]], the Baliscan government initiate a variety of programs aimed at rapidly achieving {{W|energy independence}} through sustainable means. Amongst these was the 1978 the ''Pró-Álcool'' program. The Pró-Etanol or ''Programa Nacional do Etanol'' (National Ethanol Program) was a nationwide program financed by the government to phase out all automobile fuels derived from {{W|fossil fuels}} in favour of {{W|ethanol}}, in order to prioritize fuel for {{W|diesel locomotive}}s. The program successfully reduced by 8 million the number of cars running on gasoline in Balisca, thereby reducing the country's dependence on oil imports.
[[File:Baixas HSR.png|thumb|The [[Alcántara|domestically produced]] and designed [[Alcántara|Halcón EMU]] on the [[Baliscan Railways Group|''Transoccidental'']] [[AVB]]'s  [[Macaio–Uxiu high-speed rail line|''Baixas'' route]].]]
The needs of a rapidly modernizing economy and society created a great demand for new transport and energy infrastructures. Thousands of miles of railways and highways were completed in record times across Bayara to connect the main urban areas, while {{W|dam}}s and and industrial facilities were constructed, often without regard for geological and environmental conditions. A concomitant boom of the {{W|real estate market}}, increasingly under pressure by strong demographic growth and {{W|internal migration}}s, led to the explosion of urban areas. Vast neighborhoods of {{W|social housing}} were built in the outskirts of many cities, leading over the years to severe problems of congestion, [[W:Urban decay|urban decay]] and street violence, which the community is still addressing. 

The {{W|natural environment}} was constantly under strain by unregulated industrial expansion, leading to widespread {{W|air pollution|air}} and {{W|water pollution}} and {{W|ecological disasters}} like the [[Almoriya explosion]] and the [[Baladona dam disaster]], until a green consciousness developed took hold from the 1980s. At the beginning of the 2010s Bayara has begun the process of {{W|dam removal}} to restore the natural state of rivers in accordance with {{W|indigenous decolonization}} efforts. Transportation has rapidly improved in Bayara since the end of the [[Special Period]], with Bayara receiving billions of {{W|equalization payments}} for expanding {{W|public transit}}, {{W|freeway removal}}, and the [[AVB|Baliscan high speed rail system]]. With these generous subsidies, Bayaran infrastructure has rapidly caught up with the rest of Balisca. Rising immigration, continuing urbanization and {{W|direct action|radical}} [[Ecological Action|environmentalist pressure]] has further discouraged dependence on roads and increasing shifts to rail.

Rail travel has become increasingly attractive option for Baliscans as a whole, but the shift is far more visible in Bayara. {{W|Car ownership}} has shown a rapid decline in recent years, and it is has become impractical and inconvenient to live in any large Bayaran city or metropolitan area while owning a car. However, many medium-sized towns and smaller communities lack the widespread access to public transit options that are commonplace in other Baliscan cities.

==Demographics==
{{Main|Demographics of Balisca}}

In the [[2025 Baliscan census]], Bayara was determined to have a population of 43,674,612. Its rate of population growth has been the second-highest in the Baliscan Confederation (after [[Araucaria]]) for the last decade, averaging 1.6% growth per year. High fertility and birth rates persisted until the 1970s, after which they started to decline, coinciding with the beginning of the [[Special Period]]. The population has been consistently becoming younger as a result of immigration, and the community currently has the lowest median age in the confederation, with a median age of 35.2 years. The {{W|total fertility rate}} has also climbed from an all-time low of 1.18 children per woman in 1995 to 1.8 in 2018, albeit still below the {{W|replacement rate}} of 2.1 and considerably below the high of 5.06 children born per woman in 1883. Nevertheless, the total fertility rate is expected to reach 2.1–2.3 in 2030. 

It is estimated that Bayara's population will peak at 35 million people in 2029, maintaining it's position as the most-populous community in the Confederation. About 87 percent of the community's population lives in urban areas, with more than 55 percent living in the [[Solana|Solana megalopolis]] alone.

At the Baliscan census of 2025, 72.4% of the residents of Bayara claimed [[Baliscan nationality law|Baliscan nationality]], and 65% declared that they speak [[Baliscano]] at home. At the 2020 census, 50.24% of the almost 28.4 million inhabitants of Bayara declared to be descendants of another single ancestry than [[Jacano Baliscans|Jacano]]. That number includes 1.7 million who declared to be [[Dagiti Baliscans|Dagiti]] as a national-ethnic identification (489,000 as single ethnicity and 1,200,000 a second ethnicity) and 700,000 [[Sinchewese Baliscans|Sinchewese]] (575,000 as single ethnicity).

Bayara's median life expectancy at birth is 83.4 years (85.5 for females, and 81.3 for males), which is the third-highest in Balisca.

===Ethnography and immigration===
{{Main|Immigration to Balisca}}

[[File:Asian Family in Brazil.jpg|thumb|[[Yanbaru|Yanbarun]] immigrant family in [[Macaio]] (1928)]]
A 2018 poll conducted jointly by [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística|ICS]] and the [[Instituto de Opinión Pública de Balláran]] (IOPB) estimated that the largest ethnic groups were of [[West Kaftia|West Kaftian]] origin (6 million), followed by [[Xacano people|Xacano]] (5 million), [[South Kaftia|South Kaftian]] (2-5 million), [[Volisanian Baliscans|Volisanian]] (4 million) [[Andalusian people|Andalusian]] (3.5 million), [[Beron Baliscans|Beron]] (3 million), [[Dagiti Baliscans|Dagiti]] (3 million), and [[Bizkarran people|Bizkarran]] (2 million). There are also sizable minorities of other Mesogean ethnic groups, namely Burawi and Aetolians. Bayara has a significant {{W|Romani people|Romani}} (''Gitanos'') population, numbering between 200,000 and 400,000; many foreign ''Romani'' are formerly undocumented migrants that were facing expulsion from Illypnian countries who have been granted temporary residency with a path to citizenship.

Since the arrival of the Xacano colonists in 1500, considerable genetic mixing between Indigenous, Illypnians, and Kaftians has taken place in all regions of the community: Illypnian ancestry was once dominant across Bayara, accounting for between 55% and 67% in 1980, while the Kaftian ancestry among the Bayarans are estimated that 24.30% to 35% and more than 80% of Bayarans have over 10% Kaftian ancestry. Indigenous ancestry is significant and present in all regions of Bayara.

From the 19th century, Balisca opened its borders to immigration. About five million people from over 60 countries migrated to Bayara between 1808 and 1972, most of them of Andalusian, Sudanese Kaftian ([[Ayori people|Ayori]], [[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzan]], etc.) Sartan, Xacano, Dagiti, Volisanian, Beron, Jewish, Kharani, [[Kalinovan Baliscans|Kalinovan]], [[Yanbarun Baliscans|Yanbarun]], [[Sanese Baliscans|Sanese]], [[Kwangjuan Baliscans|Kwangjuan]], and [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayani]] origin. Outside of Araucaria, Bayara also has the 3rd-largest population of Bayani ancestry in the confederation, with and estimated 3–6 million people. According to confederal [[Secretariat of International Affairs and Cooperation (Balisca)|Secretariat of International Affairs and Cooperation]], Bayara is a home to a [[TBA]] diaspora of 1 to 1.5 million, the largest [[TBA]] overseas community in the world. 

