Bayara is a [[W:Secularism|secular society]], and [[W:Freedom of religion|freedom of religion]] is a constitutional right. Like the remainder of Balisca, religious attitudes are highly influenced by the idea of [[W:Seny|''seny'']], and borrows from the French concept of ''[[W:Laïcité|laïcité]]'' ({{Lang-an|laicismo}}, literally "secularity"), a strict [[W:Separation of Church and State|separation of church and state]] under which public life is kept completely secular.
[[File:Misión de San Agustín de Xaraba (expanded).png|left|thumb|[[Misión de San Agustín de Xaraba]] in eastern [[Xaraba]]]]
[[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholicism]] has been the predominant religion in Bayara since the colonial era, though it is not nearly as actively practiced today as it was. While in the 1970s, more than 60% of the Bayarans still declared themselves to be Catholic, in the 1990s this proportion had fallen to less than 40%, in the 2000s to less than 30%, and in the 2010s it is less than 20%. According to a survey held in 2020 by [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística|ICS]] and [[Instituto de Opinión Pública de Balláran]] (IOPB) 16% of the total population of Bayara was [[W:Roman Catholic|Petran Catholic]] as of that year, with further 12% adhered to some other form of Christianity (majority [[Catharism|Cathar]]); at the same time 54% of the population had no religion ([[W:Atheism|atheism]] or [[W:Agnosticism|agnosticism]]), 18.5% were [[W:Muslim|Muslims]], 2.5% were followers of other faiths, and the remaining 0.4% were undecided about their faith.

During the [[Baliscan Revolution]], militants conducted a violent [[Dechristianisation of Bayara|campaign of de-Christianisation]] against the [[Petran Catholic Church]], aimed at destroying the influence of these institutions on society. Across the community both clergy and churches were attacked, with [[W:Iconoclasm|iconoclasm]] justifying the stripping of churches of statues, ornament, and anything considered to be of significance. By the [[1919 Confederal Convention|end of the revolution]], between 70-90% of all religious structures had been gutted or burned across Bayara, while an estimated 32,000-35,000 had been killed in anti-Catholic driven violence. 

Estimates of the number of Muslims in Bayara vary widely, but it is agreed that Bayara has the second-highest number of Muslim residents in the confederation (after [[Araucaria]]). A study made by the [[Confederal Islamic Congress]] in 2020 estimated that there were about 4.5 million inhabitants of Muslim background living in the community as of 2023, accounting for 8-14% of the total population. The vast majority was composed of immigrants and descendants originating from Central Kaftia (specifically [[Tamazgha]] and [[Kwarada]]), and other Kaftian countries like [[Ayorin]] and [[Bonande]]. 

Considering migration patterns since the beginning of the 21st century and the lack of solid figures on undocumented residents, it is widely accepted that Muslims in Bayara now make up about 15-20% of the total population (4 to 6 million). Of those who identify as Muslim, around 33% responded that they were practicing believers.

In a 2023 ICS poll, 7% of respondents answered "I believe there is a God", 16% answered "I believe there is some sort of spirit or life force", and 77% answered "I don't believe there is any sort of spirit, God or life force".
==Geography==
{{Main|Geography of Balisca}}

Bayara boasts a diverse and captivating geography that plays a crucial role in its cultural, economic, and environmental dynamics. The nation's topography is primarily characterized by the expansive [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]] (locally referred to as ''Llanures ocidental''), a sprawling expanse that claims approximately 70% of Bayara's total land area. This vast flatland is considered the earliest cradle of Bayara's identity and development, shaping its population distribution, agricultural prowess, and cultural progression.

The central region of Bayara, a relatively level terrain, stands as a focal point of human activity. Its significance extends beyond mere geography; it is the epicenter of Bayara's population, a wellspring of agricultural resources, and the cradle from which the nation burgeoned subsequent to the Jacano colonization of Balisca. This strategic location has earned Bayara the [[W:Sobriquet|sobriquet]] of the "[[W:Breadbasket|breadbasket of Balisca]]," a testament to its pivotal role in sustaining the region's culinary and nutritional needs.

The coastal fringes of Bayara delineate its maritime boundary, where the warm influence of the {{W|Brazil Current}} gives rise to the enchanting bays of [[Terranexo]], [[Serpiedra]], and [[Selvadia Bay|Selvadia]]. Along these coastal stretches, prominent slopes emerge near the water's edge, lending the landscape a remarkable juxtaposition of terrain. These slopes not only add to the aesthetic charm of Bayara's coastal regions but also harbor a diverse array of ecosystems, including the unique restinga vegetation, picturesque bays, serene lagoons, and verdant tropical forests.

